<meta charset="utf-8">
<meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width,initial-scale=1">

{# DROP `no-js` BEFORE THE FIRST PAINT. <html> ships with it so a visitor without
   JavaScript — and any crawler that doesn't run it — gets the logo's role list as a
   plain stacked list rather than an empty box (see `.no-js .b-logo__item` in
   _logo.scss, which undoes the ticker's shared grid cell).

   app.js clears it, but app.js is a module: deferred until the 144kB bundle has
   downloaded AND parsed. With critical CSS inlined the page paints long before that,
   and it painted with .no-js still applied — so every role showed at once, stacked,
   until the bundle landed and snapped them into the ticker.

   Inline and first in the head, so it runs during parse and the class is gone before
   anything is drawn. `is-loading` deliberately stays: that one is the page-transition
   machinery's and belongs to app.js, which removes both (removing an absent class is
   a no-op).

   A visitor with JS off never runs this, which is the entire point — they keep the
   fallback. #}
<script>document.documentElement.classList.remove('no-js');</script>
{# "{Page} | Jon Leverrier" on inner pages (e.g. Contact); just the name on home.

   Sections listed here get a third segment naming what the page IS, between the two:
   "Vaiie Product Branding | Case Study | Jon Leverrier". A study's title is the short
   name it's filed under, which on its own says nothing about the page in a search
   result or a tab strip. Add a handle here to give another section the same treatment —
   anything not listed keeps the plain two-part title. #}
{% set sectionLabels = {
    caseStudies: 'Case Study',
    notes: 'Notes',
} %}
{% set pageEntry = (entry is defined and entry and entry.section.handle != 'homepage') ? entry : null %}
{% set pageTitle = pageEntry
    ? [
        pageEntry.title,
        sectionLabels[pageEntry.section.handle] ?? null,
        'Jon Leverrier',
    ]|filter(part => part)|join(' | ')
    : 'Jon Leverrier' %}

{# A category page — a topic under the notes — reads as the notes section's:
   "Development | Notes | Jon Leverrier". Keyed by group handle, like the
   sections above; a group not listed keeps the two-part title. #}
{% set groupLabels = {
    topics: 'Notes',
} %}
{% if category is defined and category %}
    {% set pageTitle = [
        category.title,
        groupLabels[category.group.handle] ?? null,
        'Jon Leverrier',
    ]|filter(part => part)|join(' | ') %}
{% endif %}

{# A routed page with no element — a year of notes — names itself: it sets
   `pageTitleParts` at its top level (e.g. ['2020', 'Notes']) and the site name
   follows, as everywhere else. #}
{% if pageTitleParts is defined and pageTitleParts %}
    {% set pageTitle = (pageTitleParts|merge(['Jon Leverrier']))|join(' | ') %}
{% endif %}

{# An entry's Custom Page Title, when set, IS the title — used as written, with no
   segments added, so the author decides whether the site name follows. Any entry,
   the homepage included: that's the one page the assembled title doesn't name. #}
{# Read from the CATEGORY too (a topic page has no entry) — the same field is on
   the Topics group's SEO tab, and without this a topic's custom title was set in
   the CMS and silently ignored. #}
{% set titleElement = entry is defined and entry ? entry : (category is defined and category ? category : null) %}
{% set customTitle = titleElement ? (titleElement.seoCustomPageTitle ?? '')|trim : '' %}
{% if customTitle %}
    {% set pageTitle = customTitle %}
{% endif %}
{# A paginated page leads with its number — "Page 3 | Notes | Jon Leverrier" —
   the most specific part first, as the rest of the title is built, so pages 2
   and 3 of a listing aren't three tabs and three search results with one name. #}
{% if craft.app.request.pageNum > 1 %}
    {% set pageTitle = 'Page ' ~ craft.app.request.pageNum ~ ' | ' ~ pageTitle %}
{% endif %}
<title>{{ pageTitle }}</title>

{# Meta description, first of: the entry's own Meta Description field, its Summary
   (a case study's is called Bio — see FindContext), then the site-wide one on the
   globals single. Plain text only, so tags are stripped and the lot trimmed; when
   every rung is empty no tag is emitted rather than an empty one. #}
{# A category page — a topic — takes the same chain: its own Summary, then the
   site-wide description. #}
{% set metaEntry = entry is defined and entry ? entry : (category is defined and category ? category : null) %}
{% set metaGlobals = craft.entries().section('globals').one() %}
{% set metaCandidates = [] %}
{% if metaEntry %}
    {% set metaCandidates = [metaEntry.seoMetaDescription ?? '', metaEntry.summary ?? '', metaEntry.bio ?? ''] %}
{% endif %}
{% if metaGlobals %}
    {% set metaCandidates = metaCandidates|merge([metaGlobals.seoMetaDescription ?? '']) %}
{% endif %}
{% set metaCandidates = metaCandidates|map(v => v|striptags|trim)|filter(v => v) %}
{% set metaDescription = metaCandidates|length ? metaCandidates|first : '' %}
{% if metaDescription %}
<meta name="description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
{% endif %}
{# The element's own URL, NOT the one the visitor arrived on. Those differ by
   whatever is hanging off the end of it — a campaign tag, a share parameter, a
   stray trailing segment — and each variant would otherwise nominate itself as a
   separate page, splitting one page's ranking across however many links to it
   exist in the wild.

   `element` is what the layout already resolved (entry ?? product ?? variant),
   so this follows whatever the page is rather than assuming an entry. The
   fallback covers a template rendered without one, where the request path is the
   best available answer — `pathInfo` and not the full request URL, because it
   drops the query string, which is the whole point. #}
{% set canonical = (element is defined and element and element.url) ? element.url : siteUrl(craft.app.request.pathInfo) %}
{# A paginated page is its own page — page 3 of the notes lists different notes
   from page 1 — so it's self-canonical, at its /p3 address, never folded into
   the first page. The page number is read from the request (Craft strips the
   trigger segment from the path). prev/next link the sequence for the crawlers
   that still read them; next needs the page count, which a paginating template
   sets at its top level as `pageCount` (see _views/single/notes). #}
{% set pageNum = craft.app.request.pageNum %}
{% set pageBase = canonical %}
{% set pageTrigger = craft.app.config.general.pageTrigger ?? 'p' %}
{% if pageNum > 1 %}
    {% set canonical = pageBase ~ '/' ~ pageTrigger ~ pageNum %}
{% endif %}
<link rel="canonical" href="{{ canonical }}">
{% if pageNum > 1 %}
    <link rel="prev" href="{{ pageNum == 2 ? pageBase : pageBase ~ '/' ~ pageTrigger ~ (pageNum - 1) }}">
{% endif %}
{% if pageCount is defined and pageCount and pageNum < pageCount %}
    <link rel="next" href="{{ pageBase ~ '/' ~ pageTrigger ~ (pageNum + 1) }}">
{% endif %}
{# The notes feed, advertised on EVERY page rather than only on the notes index: this
   is how a reader discovers it — paste the site's address into a feed app and it reads
   this tag to find the feed. Hiding it on the one page someone is least likely to have
   open defeats the point. #}
<link rel="alternate" type="application/rss+xml" title="{{ siteName }} — Notes" href="{{ siteUrl('notes.rss') }}">
{# llms.txt — the site in markdown for a language model (_views/feeds/llms.twig).

   There is NO standard rel for this. llmstxt.org defines the file and its well-known
   path, not a discovery link, so anything reading it is looking at /llms.txt directly
   and would find it without this tag. `alternate` is the closest honest fit — it is an
   alternate representation of this site — and the type matches what is actually served.
   Harmless if nothing ever reads it; there if something starts to. #}
<link rel="alternate" type="text/plain" title="llms.txt" href="{{ siteUrl('llms.txt') }}">
<link rel="icon" href="{{ craft.vite.asset('build/images/favicon.ico') }}" sizes="any">

{# Open Graph and Twitter cards — what a link to this page looks like when it
   unfurls in LinkedIn, a chat, a feed. Title and description are the ones
   already built above (with the meta chain's description); the image is the
   element's own Social Image, else the first image in its content, else the
   site default on Globals (see helpers/Social), at 1200 × 630. No image at
   all → no image tags, rather than a broken one. Notes are articles; the rest
   is a website. #}
{% set socialElement = element is defined and element ? element : (category is defined and category ? category : null) %}
{% set socialImage = craft.frontend.socialImage(socialElement) %}
<meta property="og:site_name" content="Jon Leverrier">
<meta property="og:title" content="{{ pageTitle }}">
{% if metaDescription is defined and metaDescription %}
    <meta property="og:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
{% endif %}
<meta property="og:url" content="{{ canonical }}">
<meta property="og:type" content="{{ entry is defined and entry and entry.section.handle == 'notes' ? 'article' : 'website' }}">
{% if socialImage %}
    <meta property="og:image" content="{{ craft.frontend.socialImageUrl(socialImage) }}">
    <meta property="og:image:width" content="1200">
    <meta property="og:image:height" content="630">
    {% if socialImage.alt %}
        <meta property="og:image:alt" content="{{ socialImage.alt }}">
    {% endif %}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary_large_image">
{% else %}
    <meta name="twitter:card" content="summary">
{% endif %}
<meta name="twitter:title" content="{{ pageTitle }}">
{% if metaDescription is defined and metaDescription %}
    <meta name="twitter:description" content="{{ metaDescription }}">
{% endif %}

{# Has this visitor already seen this build? The cookie holds the hash of the
   bundled CSS, so it only reads true for someone who has been here since the
   last deploy — which is also the visitor whose HTTP cache still holds these
   files. Called once: a miss sets the cookie as well as reporting one. #}
{% set firstVisit = not craft.frontend.hasCriticalCssCookie() %}

{% if firstVisit %}
    {# Preloads are for the cold cache only. A returning visitor already has
       these files and preloading them again just spends early bandwidth and
       priority on requests the cache would have served anyway.

       Fonts: the four woff2 faces, both families. JonSonSans is what the h1 and
       the header button are set in, so it decides whether the first paint looks
       right. The .otf copies are woff2 fallbacks — preloading them makes every
       modern browser fetch the same face twice.

       JonSonMono used to be excluded here, on the grounds that nothing above the
       fold used it and both weights shipped as .ttf at ~115kB. Neither is true
       any more. That reasoning was written for Roboto Mono, which has since been
       replaced by Söhne — 25kB a face in woff2, SMALLER than either Baikal
       weight. And it is very much above the fold: field-input sets the ask bar's
       placeholder in it, which is the front door's one control, plus the meta
       rows on every note and case study.

       `crossorigin` is load-bearing, not decorative — fonts are fetched
       anonymously, and a preload that doesn't match the real request is a second
       download rather than a saved one. #}
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin href="{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/Baikal-ExpandedLight.woff2') }}">
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin href="{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/Baikal-ExpandedBold.woff2') }}">
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin href="{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/soehne-mono-leicht.woff2') }}">
    <link rel="preload" as="font" type="font/woff2" crossorigin href="{{ craft.vite.asset('build/fonts/soehne-mono-halbfett.woff2') }}">

    {{ craft.vite.includeCriticalCssTags(tplPath) }}
    {{ craft.vite.script('build/js/app.js', true) }}

{% else %}
    {{ craft.vite.script('build/js/app.js', false) }}
{% endif %}

{# The point cloud's geometry, homepage only — 373kB, and no other page asks for
   it. hero.js is a dynamic import, so without this the binary can't even be
   requested until the JS bundle has parsed and the chunk has loaded.

   OUTSIDE the firstVisit block above, unlike the fonts, and that difference is the
   whole point. `firstVisit` answers "has this visitor been served this build's
   CSS", which ANY page sets — and it stands in for "already cached" only for files
   every page loads. This one is requested by a single page. A visitor who arrives
   on a Jonson answer or a case study sets the cookie there, and by the time the
   logo brings them here firstVisit is false while the binary has still never been
   fetched: gated, the preload went missing on precisely the visit that needed it.

   Ungated it costs nothing on a repeat homepage view — the preload resolves out of
   the HTTP cache with no request.

   fetchpriority="low" IS WHAT KEEPS IT OUT OF THE WAY, not its position in the head.
   Being written after the fonts was meant to do that and does nothing: the preload
   scanner finds every tag in one pass, and Chrome was starting this 365kB fetch at
   HIGH priority 6ms in, alongside the fonts and the bundle — measured. Which is the
   opposite of the intention, because the homepage's LCP element is the <h1>, and the
   thing competing with it for a mobile connection was the backdrop behind it.

   Low priority rather than no preload at all: hero.js is a dynamic import, so
   without a tag here the binary cannot even be requested until the bundle has parsed
   and the chunk has loaded. This keeps the early discovery and drops the urgency.

   `as="fetch"` + `crossorigin` to match what hero.js issues: a plain fetch(),
   which is CORS mode with same-origin credentials. #}
{# The point cloud's CODE, discovered from the HTML rather than three levels into a
   chain. app.js dynamically imports hero.js, which statically imports three — so the
   browser could not learn that three exists until app.js had downloaded, parsed and
   run, and then hero.js had done the same. Lighthouse measures that as a four-link
   critical path; in bytes it meant 123kB starting its download 416ms in.

   modulepreload fixes the DISCOVERY without touching the order of execution: the
   module is fetched and parsed early and sits in the map until app.js asks for it.

   Naming hero.js is NOT enough, which is what this originally assumed. Chrome
   fetches the module named in the tag and does not walk its import graph, so three
   stayed exactly where it was — measured on prod, it was requested script-initiated
   at ~1.3s, i.e. only once app.js had downloaded, parsed and run. Preloading a
   dynamic import means naming its static imports too, which craft.frontend.chunkImports
   reads out of the manifest (the chunk is content-hashed, so it cannot be written
   out by hand without going stale silently).

   fetchpriority="low" for the same reason as the binary below: this is a backdrop.
   Early discovery is worth having; early PRIORITY would put 123kB back in front of
   the <h1>, which is the LCP element and the thing we just spent the afternoon
   getting out of the way.

   Measured on a FIRST-TIME visitor specifically — fresh profile per run, so the
   document is the 89kB one carrying 21kB of inlined critical CSS, and nothing is in
   the HTTP cache. That distinction matters and was got wrong first time round: with
   one profile reused, run 1 is a first visit and the rest are repeat visits (the
   criticalcss cookie suppresses the inlining), so the median silently reports the
   repeat visitor. 5 runs each, 1.6Mbps/150ms/4x CPU:

       three starts   1565ms (script)  ->  620ms (parser)
       FCP             780ms           ->  772ms
       LCP             852ms           ->  840ms

   So: free, but do not oversell it. LCP is unchanged — the <h1> waits on the
   document and nothing after it, which the other direction confirms (holding
   hero.js, three AND the binary back by four seconds moves LCP 896ms -> 920ms, i.e.
   not at all). And the hero does not appear any sooner either: the 293kB point-cloud
   binary gates that, and it lands 0.7-0.8s AFTER three with or without this tag, so
   the canvas is up at ~4.1-4.4s either way.

   What it actually buys is the removal of a Lighthouse critical-chain link, and the
   head start is there to be spent if the binary ever stops being the long pole. It
   costs nothing for repeat visitors, for whom the chunk is simply a cache hit. #}
{% if entry is defined and entry and entry.section.handle == 'homepage' %}
    <link rel="modulepreload" fetchpriority="low" crossorigin href="{{ craft.vite.asset('build/js/components/hero.js') }}">
    {% for url in craft.frontend.chunkImports('build/js/components/hero.js') %}
        <link rel="modulepreload" fetchpriority="low" crossorigin href="{{ url }}">
    {% endfor %}
{% endif %}

{% if entry is defined and entry and entry.section.handle == 'homepage' %}
    {# .gz, because that is the copy hero.js asks for — a preload that does not match
       the real request is a second download rather than a saved one. #}
    <link rel="preload" as="fetch" fetchpriority="low" crossorigin href="{{ craft.vite.asset('build/assets/hero.bin') }}.gz">
{% endif %}

{# COLOUR-REVEAL INITIAL STATE — inline, on every page, deliberately duplicated
   from _picture.scss.

   These three rules have to exist at FIRST PAINT or the effect cannot happen at
   all: they are what hides the image and paints the gradient in its place. If
   they arrive even a moment late, the browser has already drawn the photo, and
   there is nothing left to reveal — the image never appears to do anything.

   They can't be left to the bundle, because the bundle is not reliably there in
   time:
     · in dev, Vite serves CSS through JavaScript, so the stylesheet does not
       exist until app.js has been fetched, parsed and run. Measured on a cold
       throttled load: the image was already painted at opacity 1 while still
       downloading, because no rule had yet said otherwise.
     · in production, the inlined critical CSS is only sent on a visitor's FIRST
       page view of a build (see the cookie above). Every view after that waits
       on the external stylesheet.

   Everything else — the transitions, the staggering, `is-loaded`, `is-developed`
   — stays in _picture.scss. Only the initial state is here, because only the
   initial state is needed before the stylesheet lands. Keep the two in step. #}
<style>
    .c-picture.has-tone{position:relative}
    .c-picture.has-tone::before{content:'';position:absolute;inset:0;background-image:linear-gradient(135deg,var(--tone-a) 0,var(--tone-b) 100%)}
    .c-picture.has-tone .c-picture__img{position:relative;opacity:0}
</style>
{# Without JS nothing ever adds `is-loaded`, so the image would stay hidden behind
   its own placeholder forever.

   <noscript> and NOT the `.no-js` class that <html> carries: that class is on the
   page for every visitor and is only removed once app.js runs, which is precisely
   the window this whole block exists to cover. Keying the fallback off it made the
   image visible during exactly the moment it needed to be hidden — the effect
   couldn't fire, which is what it looked like from the outside. #}
<noscript>
    <style>.c-picture.has-tone .c-picture__img{opacity:1}</style>
</noscript>
<script>
    // Set pre-paint so the incoming page starts hidden and warps in (no flash).
    try {
        if (sessionStorage.getItem('jonson-nav')) {
            sessionStorage.removeItem('jonson-nav');
            document.documentElement.classList.add('is-page-entering');
        }
    } catch (e) {}
</script>